00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.610
Specifically, our team
is part of the Cloud AI

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.930
family of products.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.790
And we focus on
three main areas.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.650
The first one, Vision
API, and the second one,

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.380
Video Intelligence API, help
you understand your images

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.260
and videos, respectively,
using the power of Google

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.670
pre-trained models.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:29.267
And the third one,
AutoML, is a suite

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:31.850
of products that help you build
high-quality, machine learning

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.520
models with no machine
learning expertise required.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.460
Let's first start
with the Vision API.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.300
The Vision API helps you
understand your images using

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.050
Google pre-trained models.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.620
You can use this API to
extract value from your images,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.650
extract contents, and then
integrate this content

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.890
into your applications.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.320
Vision API has a rich
set of features for you

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.450
to understand your
images, ranging

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:03.910
from content annotation,
OCR, all the way,

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:05.950
to content moderation.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.230
But since the last
Google Next, I'm

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:12.590
excited to introduce
14 new capabilities.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.430
The first one,
Handwriting OCR, helps

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:18.710
you extract handwritten
text from your documents.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.610
The second one,
Object Localizer,

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.690
helps you extract not
only the presence,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.940
but the actual coordinates
of objects in your images.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.850
And the third one,
Web Detection,

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:36.290
helps you extract entities and
similar images from the web,

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:40.070
leveraging the power
of Google Search.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.440
The fourth one,
Product Search, helps

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.370
retailers integrate visual
search into their mobile

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.840
and web applications.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.980
In the next few slides, I'll
provide three example solutions

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.770
that customers are assembling
with our technology.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:59.290
In the first one,
customers are combining

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:03.310
the power of Vision API
content annotation features

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:06.820
with Cloud Sub in App Engine
to index their images at scale

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:12.100
and be able to query them
using their rich metadata.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.820
Media corporations are using
this to tag to their content

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.510
and find the right media
assets for the next production

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.800
or media campaign.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.470
Financial institutions
are using this

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:28.070
to find text in a large
set of scanned documents.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.610
And even retailers are
using this to find products

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.770
simply using visual features.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.920
The second one, customers
are combining the power

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:43.010
of Vision API OCR and
our Natural Language APIs

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:49.230
to be able to classify and
extract structure information

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.990
from their scanned documents.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.310
Companies are using this
to digitize their content

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:02.460
and enable new workflows and
business processes downstream.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:08.110
And the last one, retailers
are using our APIs

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.690
to build visual
search applications.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:13.450
And the way they're doing
that is by first, taking

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:17.980
different types of products,
like sneakers, pants,

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:22.620
or t-shirts, using our
Object Localizer API,

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.370
and then sending those crops
to the Product Search API

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:30.130
to retrieve the exact SKU of
that product in a customer's

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:31.600
camera.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.230
So in a nutshell, you can
assemble all these solutions

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:37.720
using our Vision API
with no machine learning

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.840
expertise required.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.780
Now switching gears, I wanted
to talk about the Video

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.780
Intelligence API.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.180
This API helps you annotate
and understand your videos

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.330
at scale in a short
period of time.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:56.440
You can think of Video
Intelligence API as Vision API

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.630
but heavily
optimized for videos,

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:02.520
meaning we leverage the
temporal component of videos.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.410
But as Vision API,
Video Intelligence API

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.390
has features for content
annotation like, Label

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:11.170
Detection, and content
moderation, like Safe Search,

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:15.040
but it also has
video-specific features

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.666
like Shot Change Detection.

00:06:17.666 --> 00:06:19.040
Media and
entertainment companies

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:26.864
are using this to detect vast
databases of media footage.

00:06:26.864 --> 00:06:28.280
Other types of
companies are using

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.830
this to determine the
right place and time

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.890
to place contextual ads
on the video screens.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.290
And companies are also using
this to extract rich metadata

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.430
from their videos to enhance the
quality of their recommendation

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:44.930
engines.

00:06:48.622 --> 00:06:50.330
Independent of the
uses of our customers,

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.300
we're very excited
about what they

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.900
are doing with our technology.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.930
But this is just the beginning.

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:59.050
With the next few weeks,
we'll be introducing

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.100
key new capabilities
that are going

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.310
to enhance our Video
Intelligence capabilities even

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.390
further.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:06.650
So please stay tuned.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.890
Now, if the Video Intelligence
API or the Vision API

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:15.500
do not meet your needs,
because your data belongs

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.510
to a specific domain,
or because you

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:21.080
want us to add additional
support for your own concepts,

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.020
we introduced AutoML.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:27.140
AutoML is a suite
of products that

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:29.420
helps you build high-quality
machine learning models

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.910
for your own data and for your
own use case with no machine

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.420
learning expertise required.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.310
We all know machine
learning's hard.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:40.220
Whenever you're confronted with
a machine learning challenge,

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.260
you have to determine what
is the right set of data

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.291
preprocessing thing needs to
use, which model should you

00:07:46.291 --> 00:07:46.790
use.

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.000
If you are using
deep learning, you

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.791
need to determine which
architecture to use

00:07:49.791 --> 00:07:52.510
or which set of
parameters to pick.

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:54.260
And after you're done
training your model,

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:57.020
you need to determine what is
the right server infrastructure

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:59.600
to serve these models
at scale and meeting

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.650
whatever latency
requirements you may have.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.346
This entire process is
very time consuming.

00:08:06.346 --> 00:08:08.840
It requires a lot of
money, because training

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.220
a lot of these models takes a
lot of computational resources

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:15.050
and at the same time require
significant machine learning

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.010
expertise.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:20.090
So that's the reason
why we introduce AutoML,

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:23.110
to help you automate
the most complex,

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:27.060
hardest steps when building
a machine learning model.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.020
And this way, you can focus
on higher value activities,

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:35.390
like collecting the
right data and defining

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:38.830
the right set of business
objectives for your problem.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:40.944
And all of this without
having to compromise

00:08:40.944 --> 00:08:42.110
on mission learning quality.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:48.040
So in a nutshell, AutoML helps
to build high-quality machine

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:50.330
learning models
for your own data.

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.955
And after you train your model,
we deploy this model on an API

00:08:52.955 --> 00:08:56.700
that's easy to use that you
can use to run predictions

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.310
with the rest of your data set.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:04.740
Yesterday, we introduced two new
AutoML products, AutoML Natural

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:07.820
Language and AutoML Translate.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:11.590
But the most relevant AutoML
for this particular session

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:14.700
is AutoML Vision that helps
to build high-quality image

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.840
classification models.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:18.780
So, for example, let's
imagine that you're

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.480
a retailer trying to classify
different types of products

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.700
into your own set of categories.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:26.520
All you have to do is provide a
series of examples of handbags,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.880
shoes, and hats, and AutoML
does the magic behind the scenes

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.770
to produce a model
that then you can later

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.960
use to tag the rest
of your product data.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:40.170
Now how does AutoML
work behind the scenes?

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:43.700
Behind the scenes, AutoML
uses AI to build AI.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:47.160
Every time you pass
your data to AutoML,

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.430
an AI agent builds a
high-quality, deep learning

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:54.510
model with optimal architecture
and a set of hyper-parameters

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:57.750
to solve your particular
business need.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.900
Now to test this
technology, we run

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.040
AutoML on ImageNet,
arguably the most

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.510
popular and heavily-studied
data scientist data set.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.920
And AutoML managed
to produce new,

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.020
state-of-the-art results
on these data sets.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.550
And what's even
more remarkable is

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:15.220
the fact that because these
models are produced by AI,

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.466
it can optimally trade
up the model size

00:10:17.466 --> 00:10:19.090
and accuracy of this
model, something's

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:22.330
that's crucial to deploy
these models on the Edge.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.880
So in a nutshell,
AutoML is a product

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:31.550
for citizen data
scientists and developers

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:34.890
to build high-quality models and
with a great user experience.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:38.150
And to illustrate the point
about the user experience,

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:41.660
I wanted to quickly show
you guys a demo of AutoML.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:50.570
Let's imagine that we are
all meteorologists trying

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:58.600
to classify photos of the sky
into different types of clouds,

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:00.670
which I recently
learned is actually

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:04.879
a good predictor of
future weather events.

00:11:04.879 --> 00:11:07.420
So if I'm confronted with this
problem, all I will have to do

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.670
is collect a few example
images for each one

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:11.780
of these different
types of clouds,

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:19.240
and then open AutoML and
click on a new data set.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.790
Let's say I want
to create cloud3.

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:23.540
And here I'm offered
two different options

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:24.290
to upload my data.

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:27.760
I can either upload them to
my local drive in a zip file

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:31.300
or by pointing out a Google
Cloud Storage packet.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:34.990
But since I have already
uploaded a cloud data set,

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:38.560
I'm going to go back
to the data set section

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:44.450
and pick clouds_2 that
has around 1800 images.

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:47.540
Somebody on our team
actually liked these photos.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:50.090
And here you can see all
the different steps required

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:52.280
to build the model, ranging
from images, all the way

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:53.240
to prediction.

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:56.780
And the first part, Images, has
a Google Photo Slide UI for you

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:58.220
to manage your images.

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:03.130
Here you can create
your image labels.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:05.150
And we show you your
label distribution,

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:08.350
so you can determine how to
properly balance your data set.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:11.870
So after you're done curating
your training data set,

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.512
you can move to the
next step, Train.

00:12:15.512 --> 00:12:17.220
And here we show all
the different models

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:19.469
that you have already trained
with their corresponding

00:12:19.469 --> 00:12:21.566
Precision and Recall values.

00:12:21.566 --> 00:12:22.940
If you want to
train a new model,

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:27.530
all you have to do is click
Train new model, and that's it.

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:30.650
Now if I want to learn more
about the performance of one

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:33.710
particular model, what I have to
do is click on See evaluation.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:36.920
Here, we show more advanced
metrics like the Precision

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:42.440
and Recall graphs, PR curves.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.200
And at the same time, you
can tweak this core threshold

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.620
for you to determine the ideal
trade off between Precision

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:52.400
and Recall for your
particular business uses.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:57.050
And scrolling down, you can
see the confusion matrix

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:02.085
that shows the labels that
the model is confusing.

00:13:02.085 --> 00:13:04.460
So, for instance, you can see
that the model is confusing

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.200
all the cumulus labels
with the cirus labels

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:10.420
around 23.8% of the time.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:14.390
So if you click on that cell,
we show you sample images

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:16.700
that the model is
confusing, so you

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:21.065
can make a more guided or
educated guess about which

00:13:21.065 --> 00:13:23.630
additional images
to upload to improve

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:26.376
the quality of the model
at the decision boundaries.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.000
So after you're done
curating your data sets,

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.860
and you have built a model
that you're happy with,

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:37.400
you can go to the Predict tab.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:39.570
And here we show
different options

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:44.200
how to query your model
with a simple REST API

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.200
script or a Python script.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.360
But if you're in a rush, you
can simply upload an image

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:50.440
from your hard drive.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:52.210
Here I'm going to test
uploading an image.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.720
And voila, you can see that
the model accurately predicted

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:02.940
this is a cumulus label.

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:08.500
Now switching back
to the slides.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:21.780
So to wrap up my
session, I wanted

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:24.350
to announce that since
we launched AutoML,

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:27.250
we have had more
than 18,000 sign-ups.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:31.330
So we are extremely
humbled by our traction.

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:34.660
Our customers' use cases span
multiple verticals, ranging

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:39.060
from medical imaging, to
retailers tagging product

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:42.770
images, all the way to waste
management corporations using

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:46.290
AutoML to sort out
different types of trash,

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:52.450
like landfill,
compost, or recycling.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:54.492
But independent of the
use cases, independent

00:14:54.492 --> 00:14:56.450
of the verticals and the
size of our customers,

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.190
we're extremely
happy about what they

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:00.600
are doing with our technology.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:05.880
And on that note, I wanted to
introduce Eli to the stage.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:09.618
He's the Head of Data
Science at Litterati.

00:15:09.618 --> 00:15:12.098
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:19.150
ELI THOMAS: Hi, I'm Eli.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:21.820
I'm the data scientist
at Litterati.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:28.220
We are a platform that empowers
people in cleaning the planet.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:36.380
Each year 14 billion pounds
of trash flow into our oceans,

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:42.160
four trillion cigarettes
are thrown on the ground.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:47.500
11 billion is spent in
cleaning it up in US alone.

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:55.380
Litter destroys the
environment, kills wildlife,

00:15:55.380 --> 00:16:02.410
poisons our food system, yet
strangely, there's no data.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:04.410
So we built an app.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:09.210
Using our app, each user can
take a picture of litter, say,

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.190
a Starbucks or a
Burger King cup,

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:16.320
tag it, and then dispose of it.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.680
The data that we
collect from each user

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:23.960
is their username,
what they picked up.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:30.090
A geotag tells us where, and
a timestamp tells us when.

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:33.660
And, in turn, we are
building the global database

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:36.510
for litter, the
first of its kind,

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.780
and ultimately, a
litter-free planet.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:45.210
What we understand is the
data we collect and it's

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:48.340
integrity it's critical.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:51.440
The data we collect
is used by cities,

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:56.700
nonprofit organizations, brands,
and schools across the world.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:17:00.230
For example, in San
Francisco, our data

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:05.410
generates annual tax
revenue of $4 million.

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:09.680
But we understand the problem we
are trying to solve is complex.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:13.280
So we partnered up with
Google's AutoML team.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:19.880
And what it helps us do is make
the process as easy as possible

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:23.119
for our users, so that they
can pick up more litter

00:17:23.119 --> 00:17:25.550
and our data smarter.

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:29.900
So what AutoML does, it
takes the user images, and in

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:33.410
the back end, performs
image recognition.

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:37.740
This helps us in
our data integrity.

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.830
This is just a sample of
examples of user images

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:45.450
that AutoML has tagged in the
backend with great results.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:50.910
Hands down, for us, at
Litterati, Google's AutoML

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:53.820
has proved to be
powerful, easy to use,

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:56.970
with a robust process flow.

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:00.990
Moving forward, we want to
take AutoML from the backend

00:18:00.990 --> 00:18:05.790
and put it in each user app
to improve the user tagging.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:07.980
So the user will
still take a picture.

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:10.770
AutoML will tag it in their app.

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:16.380
And then our user will help
us improve our litter database

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:19.230
by going in and adding
additional tags,

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:26.430
say, a local brand of
beer, or cola, or candy.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.510
And guess what?

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:30.180
Our Litterati
story is spreading.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:33.630
We have some amazing
partners, with United Nations

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:35.860
being the most recent.

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:38.260
And we're just getting started.

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:40.876
Thank you so much
for letting me share.

00:18:40.876 --> 00:18:43.336
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:45.205 --> 00:18:46.288
FRANCISCO URIBE: Nice job.

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:51.200
Thanks a lot, Eli.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:56.715
So now I want to introduce you
to Zain Shah, data scientist

00:18:56.715 --> 00:18:59.090
at Opendoor, to talk about
their experience using AutoML.

00:19:04.282 --> 00:19:07.260
ZAIN SHAH: Thanks, Francisco.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:08.850
So, guys, I'm Zain Shah.

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:10.990
I'm with the data
science team at Opendoor.

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:13.170
And you may be wondering
what is Opendoor?

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:15.150
Well, our mission is
to empower everyone

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:16.530
with the freedom to move.

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:21.060
What that means is instead
of doing a bunch of repairs

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:23.880
on your home, working with
an agent to list your home,

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.430
having random people show
up at odd hours of the day,

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:28.050
instead you just
go to our website,

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:30.630
tell us a bit about your
home, and we figured out

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:34.239
from our data, what we think
it will be able to sell for.

00:19:34.239 --> 00:19:36.530
And we make an offer to buy
your house for that amount,

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:38.287
an instant cash offer.

00:19:38.287 --> 00:19:40.620
If you like our offer, you
just sell it to us and relax.

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:41.994
You don't have to
do much at all.

00:19:41.994 --> 00:19:44.410
You just move on with your life.

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:46.520
Now this may sound like
a crazy pipe dream,

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.120
but we've actually made
a lot of progress so far.

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:50.545
People love what we're doing.

00:19:50.545 --> 00:19:52.420
This time last year, we
were in three cities.

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:54.040
Now we're in 10.

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:57.730
We've acquired 800,000 houses
and put them on the market.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.590
We now spend about a
billion dollars a year

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:00.790
purchasing homes.

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:02.640
And all these
numbers are growing.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.620
We're expanding very quickly.

00:20:05.620 --> 00:20:07.690
Now what does that
experience look like?

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:09.670
Well, you basically
just go to our website.

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:11.965
You tell us where you
live, and from there, we

00:20:11.965 --> 00:20:13.840
use our data to make
more informed decisions.

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.030
I can show you a bit
of what it looks like.

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:16.871
Go to our website.

00:20:16.871 --> 00:20:18.370
Tell us where you
live, like I said.

00:20:21.189 --> 00:20:22.980
And through our process,
you basically just

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.920
give us some information that
we might not automatically have.

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:29.180
And you tell us what's
going on inside your home.

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:31.010
From this, we can
dynamically calculate

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:33.593
how much we think we're going
to be able to sell the home for.

00:20:33.593 --> 00:20:35.030
And if you like
what we offer, you

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:38.060
can basically just sell your
home to us for that amount

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:39.799
and relax.

00:20:39.799 --> 00:20:41.840
You can also investigate
what our prediction was,

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:44.360
and how we came to that amount.

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:47.920
And it's a very easy experience.

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:49.717
Now from the data
science team at Opendoor,

00:20:49.717 --> 00:20:51.300
the sorts of problems
that I deal with

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.910
are pretty crucial
to the business.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.160
Problems we deal with are,
like, accurately valuing

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:57.690
homes totally sight unseen.

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:58.960
You just go to our
website and tell us a bit,

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:00.376
and we have to
figure out actually

00:21:00.376 --> 00:21:01.710
what it's going to sell for.

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:04.650
This is important for us to
actually do this at scale.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:06.630
We need to predict how
long it'll take to sell,

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:08.310
and we need to analyze
market dynamics

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:10.530
to correctly understand
what's going on in the market

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:13.730
and optimally price the homes.

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:15.830
Now, like I said, one of
our primary challenges

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:18.740
is valuing someone's home
totally sight unseen.

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:20.500
As you can see here,
these two homes,

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.410
they might look very
similar on paper.

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:26.060
But in person, it's quite
clear that the home on the left

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:27.770
is not very nice.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:31.100
So how do we know this?

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:35.005
Well, we can actually figure
out from photos, for example,

00:21:35.005 --> 00:21:35.880
how nice the home is.

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:38.380
There are a bunch of
things that are, maybe

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:40.330
not what drives the
value of the home,

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:42.899
but definitely are strong
indicators that we've isolated.

00:21:42.899 --> 00:21:44.440
They're mostly proxy
features, things

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:45.970
like if you have stainless
steel appliances.

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:47.290
That's a pretty good indicator.

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:48.880
If you have granite
counter-tops,

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:50.200
that's a pretty good indicator.

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:53.680
Brand-new cabinets, separate
tub and shower in your bathroom,

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:56.080
things like that.

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:58.916
Now like you saw in the flow, we
get some data from our sellers.

00:21:58.916 --> 00:22:01.290
They tell us exactly a lot of
the structured information,

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:03.480
like whether they have
granite counter-tops.

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:05.010
But for the historical
transactions

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:07.470
that we train our models on,
we don't get that at all.

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:10.110
We get the square footage,
number of bathrooms,

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:12.720
maybe how many stories the home
has, and where it's located.

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:15.600
But none of this information
that we really need.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.615
So that's where computer
vision comes in.

00:22:17.615 --> 00:22:18.990
From the historical
transactions,

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:21.365
we don't have the structured
data, but we do have photos.

00:22:21.365 --> 00:22:23.190
So we can look at the
photos of the home.

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:26.306
We can figure out whether it has
an upgraded kitchen, whether it

00:22:26.306 --> 00:22:28.680
has a nice bathroom, and we
can use that to better inform

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:30.044
our decisions.

00:22:30.044 --> 00:22:31.710
But the problem there
is that's actually

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:33.880
pretty time-consuming
and difficult.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:35.460
So that's me on the right there.

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:37.740
In a past life, I've
spent weeks poring

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:40.020
over filtered visualizations
from convolutional neural

00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:41.686
networks to understand
what was going on

00:22:41.686 --> 00:22:43.490
and improve the performance.

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:44.494
But it's a lot of work.

00:22:44.494 --> 00:22:46.410
And sometimes it doesn't
even work super well,

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:47.940
and it takes way too long.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:49.940
So that's where AutoML comes in.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:54.770
Basically, all we had to do
in order to test this use case

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:57.260
is get a bunch of
labels in a spreadsheet,

00:22:57.260 --> 00:22:59.920
upload them to
Google Cloud AutoML,

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:02.510
and from there, we get a
perfectly-- well, very accurate

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:06.039
model that predicts exactly
what we want, in this case

00:23:06.039 --> 00:23:08.330
whether a kitchen has stainless
steel appliances or not

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:10.860
with basically zero work.

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:11.850
What's next for us?

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:14.230
Well, we want to
get more labels.

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:15.980
The more we know about
a home, the better.

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:19.290
So this means things like
features about the bathrooms,

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:22.910
living rooms, backyards, and
other imagery sources as well.

00:23:22.910 --> 00:23:23.910
AutoML is very flexible.

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:25.980
So we can look at aerial
imagery, different rooms,

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:27.810
even Street View images to
understand what a home looks

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:29.095
like from the outside.

00:23:29.095 --> 00:23:31.075
Thanks.

00:23:31.075 --> 00:23:34.045
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:38.864
FRANCISCO URIBE: Awesome.

00:23:38.864 --> 00:23:39.530
Thank you, Zain.

00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:43.060
And now we have Rob
Munro, CEO of Figure Eight

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:45.694
to talk about our human
labeling partnership.

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.300
ROBERT MUNRO: Thank
you, Francisco.

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:55.520
So something that all of
these AutoML use cases

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:57.710
have in common, which
is different from a lot

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:01.400
of the existing,
off-the-shelf APIs in GCP,

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:03.950
is that they rely
on human label data.

00:24:03.950 --> 00:24:07.250
So it's humans who are going in
and saying this label applies

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:08.540
to this image.

00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:12.900
And then that powers the machine
learning to do this at scale.

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:13.400
So

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:16.580
Figure Eight is a company
that provides software

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:18.930
to label your data
for machine learning.

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:20.690
We're the most
widely used software

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.765
for GCP customers today.

00:24:22.765 --> 00:24:24.140
And it was delightful
to find out

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:26.223
as we're organizing this
that Opendoor is actually

00:24:26.223 --> 00:24:27.120
one of our customers.

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:30.440
So those images that you saw
in the previous presentation

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:33.210
are ones that our
workforce labeled for them.

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:36.290
And you can find this
use case on our website.

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:40.160
So if you need to have
humans label data for machine

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:41.960
learning, there's
a number of things

00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:44.430
that you need to
think carefully about.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:46.280
First of all, how can
you make this process

00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:47.820
as efficient as possible.

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:50.480
How can you have a smart
interface that allows someone

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:53.750
to create the right labels
as quickly as possible

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:55.774
and remove a lot
of the redundancy?

00:24:55.774 --> 00:24:57.440
Secondly, you need
to think about who is

00:24:57.440 --> 00:24:59.510
the right workforce for this.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:02.060
If you have a volume of labels
where you can't put your data

00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:04.580
scientists on this
manual task, then you

00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:08.060
might want to reach out
and use crowd source

00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:10.730
or professional labelers.

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:13.940
We manage a marketplace
of about 100,000 people

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:16.340
who regularly annotate
data for all kinds

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:17.850
of different information.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:20.210
Finally, you want good
machine learning integration.

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:23.990
So you want to be able to
take your human label data,

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:28.250
put it into your AutoML models,
and also quickly iterate

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:30.810
as you need to add more data.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:33.010
So one particular use
case I'm looking at,

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:34.970
which I'll speak
about here, takes us

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:38.300
from the first presentation,
which was about computer vision

00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:41.360
across the entire world, the
second presentation, which

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:43.490
was about computer
vision for your home.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:45.170
I'm going to go
inside your home,

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:49.310
specifically, into your closets,
and talk about your shoes.

00:25:49.310 --> 00:25:52.680
Who here is familiar with WGSN?

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:53.180
Anyone?

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:55.951
OK, look around at the people
with their hands up next

00:25:55.951 --> 00:25:56.450
to you.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:26:00.130
They are the best dressed
people in this room.

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:02.128
I just saw a bunch
of extra hands go up.

00:26:02.128 --> 00:26:03.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:08.210
So WGSN are a company that
predicts fashion trends.

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:10.880
One very important part
of making this forecast

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:12.950
is to see what is
currently being

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:15.980
sold across a very large
number of different retailers'

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:16.880
websites.

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:18.380
And as you can
imagine, these trends

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:21.230
are much more fine-grained
than just shoes, versus shirts,

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:22.520
versus dresses.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:26.150
It's about very specific
types and stylings

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:28.370
within these different
pieces of clothing.

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:31.020
So you can see in
this example here,

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:33.230
this is something that
a human might look at.

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:33.860
Is this a show?

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:34.940
Yes, it is.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:36.410
What kind of shoe is it?

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:39.200
Is it is it a heel,
or is it a flat shoe?

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:42.560
And you can see that
the annotator here,

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:44.690
the human creating
this annotation,

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:47.540
they can see an example of
what a flat versus a heel

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:49.940
is, with a text
description of that inline,

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:52.580
allowing them to quickly use
those instructions to make

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:54.110
the right decision.

00:26:54.110 --> 00:26:56.840
Similarly, here, if
they selected a boot,

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:58.520
this is a categorical
distinction

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:02.390
between a lace-up boot
and a slip-on boot.

00:27:02.390 --> 00:27:04.910
So this kind of interface
is one of the ways

00:27:04.910 --> 00:27:08.510
in which you're able to get
high-quality training data.

00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:10.132
Because you know
that even if it's not

00:27:10.132 --> 00:27:12.590
someone doing the labeling that
you're interacting directly

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:14.930
with, you're able
to provide them

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:17.990
with the right instructions.

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:20.660
So once these data
points have all

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:22.970
been labeled according
to their categories,

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:25.040
you can take this
information automatically

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:29.520
put it into AutoML and have
this process run at scale.

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:31.610
One of the nice
features of AutoML

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:34.569
is that it will tell you the
confidence of each prediction,

00:27:34.569 --> 00:27:36.860
and it has a really good
correlation between confidence

00:27:36.860 --> 00:27:38.170
and accuracy.

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:41.150
So what this means
for someone like WGSN,

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:42.830
or someone with a
similar use case,

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:45.800
is that they can use AutoML
to automate everything

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:48.920
that they know is correct
by machine learning.

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:50.450
And then for those
remaining items,

00:27:50.450 --> 00:27:52.610
because they need to
get 100% accuracy,

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:54.870
they can put those back
to humans for review.

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:57.500
So minimizing that human
part and optimizing

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:02.600
as much as they can, so they can
have these accurate forecasts.

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:05.404
So this is what the Figure Eight
platform looks like to do this.

00:28:05.404 --> 00:28:07.070
I'm not going to go
into all the details

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:09.050
here, just a couple of points.

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:12.050
In addition to having
configurable interfaces that

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:14.720
allows you to set these
instructions correctly,

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:16.670
there are a number
of quality controls.

00:28:16.670 --> 00:28:19.130
So, for example,
you can set which

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:21.260
workforces work on this task.

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:23.692
We work with a lot
of retail companies.

00:28:23.692 --> 00:28:25.900
So eBay that you saw earlier
is one of our customers,

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:29.090
is someone that we do
image labeling for.

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:30.590
So the same people
who have learned

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:32.600
about different
fashions through eBay

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:36.020
can also be working on
your particular task.

00:28:36.020 --> 00:28:38.476
You can automatically give
the task to multiple people,

00:28:38.476 --> 00:28:40.100
so you get a certain
level of agreement

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:42.020
for items that
might be ambiguous.

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:43.790
And you can also use
our patented system

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:45.350
for embedding test questions.

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:47.349
So if there are some items
for which you already

00:28:47.349 --> 00:28:50.840
know the labeled answers, they
can be hidden along the way,

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:52.710
and thereby, you're
evaluating the accuracy

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:55.224
of every single person that's
working on this platform.

00:28:55.224 --> 00:28:56.640
We'll often have
tens of thousands

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:00.726
of people working in parallel
for a single client's use case.

00:29:00.726 --> 00:29:02.100
It's this kind of
technology that

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:04.160
enables you to scale to
that kind of workforce

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:05.850
very seamlessly.

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:08.670
The final thing, which
is very important,

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:11.779
is making this annotation
as accurate as possible.

00:29:11.779 --> 00:29:14.320
And this is somewhere where you
can combine machine learning.

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:18.590
And this is best demonstrated as
a video rather than me talking.

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:20.550
So you can see here
machine-assisted video

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:23.310
annotation of two
methods side-by-side,

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:25.879
the bottom being the one that
you can use in our platform.

00:29:25.879 --> 00:29:27.420
So you can imagine,
all right, if you

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:32.400
have 30 frames a second times
10 seconds, that's 300 frames.

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:35.310
Then times 20 of our 6,000
individual objects that you

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:37.020
might want to identify.

00:29:37.020 --> 00:29:39.750
So what we have in
our system is a way

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:41.610
that these objects
are automatically

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:45.810
tracked between frames, still
allowing that human annotator

00:29:45.810 --> 00:29:48.630
to review every single
one and only make

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:51.630
the update, the correction, or
the deletion when the machine

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:53.502
learning has gotten this wrong.

00:29:53.502 --> 00:29:54.960
So in this particular
use case, you

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:57.240
can see we're about
25 to 30 times

00:29:57.240 --> 00:30:00.030
faster with no loss of accuracy.

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:02.400
And we'll see this be
up to 100 times faster

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.500
for a lot of our customers.

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:07.530
What that means is that the
human component creating data

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:10.140
could be reduced
to 1%, or you could

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:16.410
annotate 100 times more data, or
apply 100 times more use cases

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:17.514
for that budget.

00:30:17.514 --> 00:30:18.930
And this could be
either if you're

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:22.260
using the workforce in our
marketplace or the efficiency

00:30:22.260 --> 00:30:23.730
gains that you're
getting if you're

00:30:23.730 --> 00:30:28.170
bringing your own workforce
to this particular task.

00:30:28.170 --> 00:30:32.090
So we're really proud to
announce today the Figure Eight

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:35.090
and Google Cloud
partnership for AutoML,

00:30:35.090 --> 00:30:38.510
so that anyone using AutoML can
take advantage of the Figure

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:39.680
Eight annotation platform.

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:41.510
There's four
particular components

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:44.330
to this, our customizable
annotation template.

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:46.700
So that same
interface that you saw

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:49.310
for classifying
types of clothes can

00:30:49.310 --> 00:30:51.620
be used for any kind of
computer vision task.

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:55.550
And you can use both
our inbuilt workflows

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:57.890
and JavaScript to customize
that as much as you

00:30:57.890 --> 00:30:59.320
like to your use case.

00:30:59.320 --> 00:31:02.019
Assigned consultants,
so if you are

00:31:02.019 --> 00:31:03.560
new to trying to
get quality training

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:05.750
data from a distributed
human workforce,

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:07.970
our consultants can
help you with that.

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:11.570
Human labeling, so like I said,
access to about 100,000 people

00:31:11.570 --> 00:31:14.510
in our marketplace, both
crowd-sourced workers

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:17.750
and professionals that you can
interact with directly and get

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:19.107
paid by the hour.

00:31:19.107 --> 00:31:21.440
And quality control, the three
different quality control

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:23.990
methods that I
spoke about today.

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:25.550
So I'd love to
hand this back over

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:30.654
to Francisco who can talk
more about this partnership.

